# community — every variable this service reads, and nothing else.
#
# Rule 9 of docs/ecosystem/03 §2: a repository declares the variables it needs; the deploy provides
# exactly those. `src/env.ts` is the single place they are named in code, and `env.test.ts` asserts
# that this file and that file agree in BOTH directions — a variable here that nothing reads fails
# the suite just as loudly as one that is read and not declared.
#
# ─── EVERY SECRET BELOW IS `CHANGE_ME`, AND `CHANGE_ME` DOES NOT BOOT ─────────────────────────────
#
# That is enforced, not advisory: `env.ts` refuses a known placeholder outright and refuses anything
# shorter than 24 characters. A default secret in source is not convenient, it is catastrophic, and
# a placeholder that boots is a placeholder that reaches production.
#
# Generate real ones with:  openssl rand -hex 32
#
# ─── WHAT THIS SERVICE DELIBERATELY HAS NO VARIABLE FOR ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THERE IS NO VARIABLE THAT CAN SHORTEN OR BYPASS A TIMELOCK. Not a shorter minimum, not a skip
# flag, not a "development mode" that disables one. A deploy that could lower a governance guarantee
# is a deploy that can spend a community's treasury on somebody's say-so, and the whole of AD-15 is
# that no single party can do that. The floor lives in `src/proposals.ts` as a constant; the
# timelock itself is a column on the proposal and a BEFORE INSERT trigger on `executions`. Nothing
# in the environment touches either, and `env.test.ts` asserts that by pattern — any variable whose
# name contains TIMELOCK, SKIP_, BYPASS, OVERRIDE, DISABLE or FORCE fails the suite.
#
# There is no COMMUNITY_ADMIN_TOKEN and no break-glass credential either. The equivalent here would
# be a static string that can execute a treasury spend, which is the shape of the omnipotent service
# tokens SD-05 exists to retire.

# ─── the service ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=4000
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
# The image tag, stamped by the deploy. Appears on every log line as `version`.
CLOUDSFORGE_TAG=dev
# Identifies this replica in `jobs.locked_by`. Defaults to the hostname, which is right in a
# container and wrong on a laptop running two copies.
INSTANCE_ID=community-local

# ─── the one database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Rule 1: one database, named by this service's own variable. CI greps for any other
# `*_DATABASE_URL` token in source, so a second one fails the build rather than review.
COMMUNITY_DATABASE_URL=postgres://community:CHANGE_ME_DB_PASSWORD@postgres:5432/community

# ── SERVING BOTH NETWORKS FROM ONE POD ────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Both OPTIONAL, and both empty here on purpose: a development stack is one
# network. See micro-deploy `docs/network-consolidation.md`.
#
# Set the second DSN and this deployment holds a handle per network, choosing
# per request from the `CF-Network` header the gateway stamps. Leave it empty and
# `networkSql` holds one handle and REFUSES a testnet request rather than
# answering it out of mainnet rows.
COMMUNITY_DATABASE_URL_TESTNET=
# ── WHICH ESTATE THIS DEPLOYMENT IS ───────────────────────────────────────────
#
# REQUIRED in the estate, and the render sets it for every pod. It was once
# documented here as "never in production" and that was wrong twice over:
#
#   1. The pod keys its own database handle by it. Without it a testnet
#      deployment registers its testnet DSN under the name `mainnet` and then
#      refuses every request stamped `CF-Network: testnet`.
#
#   2. SERVICE-TO-SERVICE CALLS NEVER REACH THE GATEWAY. An outbox relay POSTing
#      to another service goes container to container; nothing stamps a header
#      on it and nothing can. Without a declared network those answer 500.
#
# The header still WINS when present, so this cannot mask a mis-stamped request
# from outside — it only answers the internal callers that never had one. Leave
# it empty only where a pod genuinely serves both estates.
CF_NETWORK_SINGLE=

COMMUNITY_DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# ─── identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Verifying the member's JWT. Authority WITHIN a community is still a `memberships` row in this
# database, read on the request that needs it — identity is asked no question about that.
IDENTITY_JWKS_URL=http://identity:4000/.well-known/jwks.json
IDENTITY_ISSUER=http://identity:4000
#
# Where identity is DIALLED, for `POST /service-tokens/exchange`. Leave it empty: it defaults to
# IDENTITY_ISSUER, which is already set everywhere this service runs, and the issuer of a token is
# by definition where that token came from. Deriving keeps the two in step — a deployment that
# exchanged a credential against one identity while trusting the JWKS of another would fail with a
# signature error nobody would read as a configuration mistake. Set it only where the two genuinely
# differ: an issuer published under a public name and dialled internally.
IDENTITY_URL=

# ─── signing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Secrets accepted on POST /v1/events, the internal inbox, newest first.
#
# A LIST, not a value, because rotation without an overlap window means every producer must change
# secret in the same instant this service does — and that instant does not exist during a rolling
# deploy. Comma-separated; duplicates are refused, because a duplicated secret makes "which key
# verified this" ambiguous, and that answer is what tells an operator a rotation has finished.
#
# That route consumes `identity.user.deleted`, which pseudonymises a member's governance record.
# Unsigned, it is an erase-anybody's-membership endpoint reachable by anything on the app network.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 48
#
# The boot guard checks the SHAPE of each entry rather than a list of known-bad strings: base64 or
# hex only (no hyphens — every placeholder this estate ever wrote had one), at least 32 DECODED
# BYTES rather than 24 keystrokes, and a measured entropy floor. So a typed value is refused however
# long it is, and there is no NODE_ENV exemption (micro-org #142). Every entry of the list faces
# that same bar, including the one on its way OUT of a rotation.
COMMUNITY_INGEST_SECRETS=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_base64_48

# Signs this service's OWN outbox deliveries to internal subscribers. Same shape check, same
# command: openssl rand -base64 48
OUTBOX_SIGNING_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_run_openssl_rand_base64_48

# ─── the credential this service presents upstream ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# One service identity, presented to the ledger, to policy and to the indexer. The scopes it must
# be minted with are named in source: `ledger:post` (ledgerclient.ts), `policy:decide`
# (policyclient.ts) and `indexer:read` (gating.ts) — exactly those and no wildcard.
#
# ─── SET THIS ONE. IT IS A CREDENTIAL, NOT A TOKEN, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS micro-org #222 ─────────
#
# `cfsc_…`, long-lived and revocable. `ServiceTokenProvider` exchanges it at identity's
# `POST /service-tokens/exchange` for an ordinary 600-second token and refreshes at a jittered 80%
# of that life, on traffic, with no timer. The exchange consumes nothing, so every replica boots
# from this one value and a restart days later still works.
#
# THE 600 SECONDS IS DELIBERATELY UNCHANGED. Rotation IS expiry; a longer TTL is the same defect
# arriving later and hurting more. Measured on the live estate on 2026-08-05, the variable BELOW
# held a JWT that had been expired for 26 HOURS on a container reporting healthy — `/livez` needs no
# upstream, so nothing in the estate ever presented the credential to anybody.
#
# What that costs here is not a failed call. A 401 is a 4xx, so `policyclient.ts` reads it as policy
# DECIDING and answers deny/`policy_401`; `executions.ts` turns that into `SpendRefusedError`; and
# `jobs.ts` SWALLOWS a refusal, because a refusal is an answer. So a community that voted, waited
# out its timelock and passed a spend is told the platform refused it, by a gate that was never
# asked — permanently, and with nothing naming the cause.
#
#   deploy/scripts/estate-bootstrap.sh already mints this into the estate's tokens.env.
COMMUNITY_IDENTITY_CREDENTIAL=CHANGE_ME_a_cfsc_credential_from_estate_bootstrap
#
# ─── VESTIGIAL. LEAVE IT UNSET. ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A pre-minted token, read once at boot and never renewable — the seam above replaces it. It exists
# only so a container redeployed with this image before its environment gains the credential starts
# degraded and LOUD (`index.ts` logs `fatal` naming exactly what will break) rather than refusing to
# boot. The credential always wins when both are set.
#
# It now faces the same shape check, so the JWT that was actually deployed under it is refused at
# boot with a line naming the ten-minute life. Empty is the correct value, and this variable is
# deleted from `env.ts` once no deployment sets it.
COMMUNITY_SERVICE_CREDENTIAL=

# ─── the ledger ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A HARD dependency (07-dependency-map.md:137, `money-write`): a treasury spend IS a ledger posting,
# and this service holds no money. There is no version of an execution that does not call it.
#
# The route is `POST /entries` — micro-ledger serves no `/v1` prefix. `clients.test.ts` asserts the
# path against ledger's real route table rather than against a memory of it.
LEDGER_BASE_URL=http://ledger:4000
LEDGER_DEADLINE_MS=5000

# ─── policy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A HARD, FAIL-CLOSED dependency (07-dependency-map.md:140) for treasury spend approval. Unreachable
# means the spend does NOT happen: the job fails, the proposal stays `timelocked`, and it is
# retried. Nothing is spent while we do not know whether it should be.
#
# See src/policyclient.ts and the README for what had to be done about policy having no
# `community.*` action and no `community:` subject arm.
POLICY_BASE_URL=http://policy:4000
POLICY_DEADLINE_MS=3000

# ─── the indexer, for token-gated membership ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL, and its absence is a SUPPORTED mode rather than a misconfiguration. With no URL the
# holdings oracle answers "unknown", and an unknown holding never demotes anybody — see
# src/gating.ts for why that is the only safe default while micro-indexer has no balance route.
#
# Leave it unset unless the indexer has grown one. `community_gate_checks_total{outcome="unknown"}`
# is the metric that says token gating is not actually running.
INDEXER_BASE_URL=
INDEXER_NETWORK=mainnet
INDEXER_DEADLINE_MS=5000

# ─── token gating ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How often a token-gated membership is re-checked, and how many per pass. A member found short is
# given a grace period (per-community, `gate_grace_hours`) and demoted only on a later pass that
# finds them still short — demoting on the first bad read would turn one indexer hiccup into an
# eviction.
COMMUNITY_GATE_RECHECK_INTERVAL_HOURS=6
COMMUNITY_GATE_RECHECK_BATCH_SIZE=100

# ─── retention ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How long an idempotency claim survives. The floor is the whole safety argument: expiring a key
# EARLY means the next replay of it runs the work again, and on the execute route that is a second
# treasury spend. A claim that produced an artefact is kept regardless of age.
COMMUNITY_IDEMPOTENCY_TTL_DAYS=30
